  <language>en_US</language>
  <keyword>quantile</keyword>
  <short_description>Quantiles of a data set.</short_description>
  <syntax>
    <syntax_item>Q = quantile(A, p)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>Q = quantile(A, n)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>Q = quantile(A, p, dim)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>Q = quantile(A, p, vecdim)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>Q = quantile(A, p, 'all')</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>Q = quantile(..., 'Method', method)</syntax_item>
  </syntax>
  <description>
    <p><b>quantile</b> returns quantiles for probabilities in the interval [0,1]. If the second input is an integer greater than one, it is interpreted as the number of evenly spaced quantiles.</p>
    <p><b>NaN</b> values are omitted. Supported methods are <b>midpoint</b>, <b>exact</b>, <b>inclusive</b>, <b>exclusive</b>, and <b>approximate</b>.</p>
    <p><b>A</b> can be a real numeric array, a <b>datetime</b> array or a <b>duration</b> array. For datetime or duration input data, the quantiles have the same class and Format as <b>A</b>, and <b>NaT</b> values are omitted like <b>NaN</b> values.</p>
  </description>
  <examples>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[A = [2 5 6 10 11 13];
Q = quantile(A, [0.25 0.5 0.75])]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description>datetime and duration input data</example_item_description>
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[d = hours([1 2 3 4 10]);
Q = quantile(d, [0.25 0.5 0.75])]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
  </examples>
